# 🚀 .NET Developer Roadmap (6–7 Months) — Interview Focused

## 🎯 Goal

Become a **job-ready .NET Developer** capable of clearing interviews for **product companies / high-paying roles**.

---

# 📅 Phase 0: Fundamentals (Week 1–2)

## 🔹 Programming Basics (if weak)

* Variables, Data Types, Operators
* Control Flow (if/else, loops)
* Functions, Recursion basics
* Arrays, Strings

## 🔹 OOP Concepts (VERY IMPORTANT)

* Classes & Objects
* Encapsulation
* Inheritance
* Polymorphism (compile-time & runtime)
* Abstraction
* SOLID Principles (interview favorite)

## 🔹 Data Structures (Core)

* Arrays, Linked Lists
* Stack, Queue
* Hashing (Dictionary)
* Basic Trees

---

# 📅 Phase 1: C# Deep Dive (Week 3–5)

## 🔹 C# Core Concepts

* CLR, CTS, CLS
* Value vs Reference Types
* Boxing / Unboxing
* Garbage Collection

## 🔹 Advanced C#

* Delegates
* Events
* Lambda Expressions
* LINQ (VERY IMPORTANT)

  * Select, Where, GroupBy, Join
* Async/Await (must know deeply)
* Exception Handling

## 🔹 Collections

* List, Dictionary, HashSet
* IEnumerable vs IQueryable

## 🔹 Interview Focus

* Difference-based questions
* Code output questions
* Memory management

---

# 📅 Phase 2: .NET Core & Backend (Week 6–10)

## 🔹 .NET Core Basics

* What is .NET Core?
* .NET Framework vs .NET Core vs .NET 6/7/8
* Project Structure

## 🔹 ASP.NET Core (VERY IMPORTANT)

* Middleware
* Dependency Injection (CRITICAL)
* Configuration
* Logging

## 🔹 Web API Development

* REST API principles
* Controllers & Routing
* Model Binding
* Validation
* Filters

## 🔹 Authentication & Authorization

* JWT Authentication
* Role-based Authorization

## 🔹 Interview Focus

* Lifecycle of request in ASP.NET
* DI container explanation
* Middleware pipeline

---

# 📅 Phase 3: Database + EF Core (Week 11–13)

## 🔹 SQL (MUST MASTER)

* Joins (Inner, Left, Right)
* Indexes
* Normalization
* Stored Procedures
* Transactions

## 🔹 Entity Framework Core

* Code First vs DB First
* Migrations
* Relationships (1-1, 1-many, many-many)
* Lazy vs Eager Loading
* LINQ to Entities

## 🔹 Performance

* Query optimization
* Avoid N+1 problem

## 🔹 Interview Focus

* Write SQL queries live
* EF vs Dapper
* Tracking vs NoTracking

---

# 📅 Phase 4: Advanced Backend + System Design (Week 14–18)

## 🔹 Advanced Topics

* Caching (MemoryCache, Redis basics)
* Background Jobs (Hangfire)
* API Versioning
* Rate Limiting

## 🔹 Design Patterns (HIGHLY IMPORTANT)

* Singleton
* Repository Pattern
* Unit of Work
* Factory Pattern
* CQRS (basic understanding)

## 🔹 System Design Basics

* Monolith vs Microservices
* API Gateway
* Load Balancing
* Scalability concepts

## 🔹 Interview Focus

* Design a scalable API
* Explain architecture decisions

---

# 📅 Phase 5: Frontend Basics (Optional but Helpful) (Week 19–20)

## 🔹 Basics

* HTML, CSS
* JavaScript fundamentals

## 🔹 One Framework (choose one)

* Angular OR React

## 🔹 Focus

* API integration
* CRUD UI

---

# 📅 Phase 6: DevOps + Deployment (Week 21–24)

## 🔹 Tools

* Git (VERY IMPORTANT)
* GitHub workflow

## 🔹 CI/CD Basics

* Build pipelines
* Deployment concepts

## 🔹 Deployment

* IIS Hosting
* Azure basics (App Service, DB)

## 🔹 Docker (Bonus)

* Container basics
* Dockerizing .NET app

---

# 📅 Phase 7: Projects (Parallel — START EARLY)

## 🔹 Must Build (Portfolio Ready)

1. **E-Commerce API**

   * Auth + Cart + Orders
   * Payment simulation

2. **Employee Management System**

   * CRUD + Role-based access

3. **Microservice Project (Advanced)**

   * 2–3 services
   * API Gateway

## 🔹 Add to Projects

* Logging
* Caching
* Error handling
* Clean architecture

---

# 📅 Phase 8: Interview Preparation (Last 3–4 Weeks)

## 🔹 DSA (Daily)

* Arrays, Strings
* Sliding Window
* Recursion basics

## 🔹 .NET Interview Questions

* DI deep dive
* Async/Await internals
* Middleware flow
* EF Core behavior

## 🔹 Mock Interviews

* Explain projects clearly
* Whiteboard coding

---

# ⚡ Daily Study Plan (Strict)

* 2 hrs → Core Concepts
* 2 hrs → Coding / Practice
* 2 hrs → Project Work

---

# 🚨 What Interviewers REALLY Check

* Can you **explain concepts clearly**
* Do you understand **real-world usage**
* Can you **debug issues**
* Are your projects **production-like**

---

# 🔥 Final Advice

* Don’t just watch tutorials → **Build**
* Focus on **depth over breadth**
* Revise concepts weekly
* Practice explaining everything aloud

---

# 🏁 Expected Outcome

After 6–7 months:

* Strong backend developer
* Ready for **mid-level interviews**
* Can target **high-paying roles**

---
